+`sight_all` gives the same views without copying them. It fills a reusable
+`Sightings` buffer and returns a `Sighted` that borrows both the world and the
+buffer. `get(slot)` is `None` for dead slots; otherwise it returns an
+`ObservationRef` whose `me()`, `opponents()`, `food()` and `prey()` borrow from
+the world at full precision, listed in the same order as in `Observation`.
+`time()`, `arena()` and `vision_radius()` complete the view, and
+`to_observation()` copies it into an owned `Observation` equal to
+`observe(slot)`. `sight_selected(&mask, &mut buffer)` takes the same masks as
+`observe_selected`. The world cannot step while a view is in use, so a view
+always describes the world it borrows:
+
+```rust
+let mut buffer = Sightings::default();
+loop {
+    let sighted = world.sight_all(&mut buffer);
+    let actions: Vec<Option<Action>> = sighted
+        .iter()
+        .map(|view| view.map(|view| decide(view.me(), view.opponents(), view.food())))
+        .collect();
+    world.step(&actions)?;
+}
+```
+
+Skipping the copies does not always make a bot faster: reading items through
+world indices costs more than reading a contiguous copy. A bot that reads every
+slot's nearest visible food and opponent head ran 14% faster with `sight_all`
+than with `observe_all_into` in a dense 96-snake arena, and 3% slower in an
+official 128-snake world, where filtering thousands of pellets dominates
+and copying them is cheap. These are the `-nearest-` [performance
+workloads](performance-workloads.md) on one thread, on Windows.
